  file = {PDF:/Users/corywhitney/Zotero/storage/L3YGGPX4/Phuong et al. - 2026 - School food environment policies in Vietnam a narrative literature and policy review of policy doma.pdf:application/pdf},
  abstract = {Objective: To conduct a narrative review and synthesize existing policies and evidence on the school food environment in Vietnam, with a focus on policy frameworks, intervention domains, and implementation mechanisms, and to identify key policy and evidence gaps to inform more coherent and effective policy development.
Methods: A narrative literature and policy review was conducted, integrating peer-reviewed studies from Scopus and Web of Science with national policy documents related to school nutrition, food safety, and health promotion. Sources were purposively selected based on their relevance to the analytical framework and synthesized narratively. The synthesis was guided by a policy-oriented analytical framework that combined international guidance on healthy school food environments with key intervention domains.
Results: Vietnam's school food environment is governed by a multi-layered policy framework, that includes political directions, foundational legislation, and subordinate legal instruments, and operationalized through multiple stakeholders. Four main policy domains were identified: capacity-building, nutritional standards, food environment regulation, and physical activity promotion. Evidence was more consistent for interventions targeting dietary quality, whereas evidence related to capacity-building and regulatory approaches remained limited and context-dependent. Three broad categories of gaps emerged: policy scope gaps related to the regulation of the external food environment, food marketing, and fiscal measures; implementation gaps, including inconsistent enforcement and urban–rural heterogeneity with implications for equity; and evidence gaps, particularly the limited availability of Vietnam-specific cost and cost-effectiveness data.
Conclusion: Vietnam has established a comprehensive policy framework for school food environments; however, the strength of supporting evidence and consistency of implementation remain uneven across domains. Strengthening intersectoral coordination, implementation capacity, regulation of the external food environment, and Vietnam-specific evidence will be essential for more equitable and scalable policy development.},
  language = {en},
  shorttitle = {School food environment policies in Vietnam},
